Llama Chess is a Console/Curses PGN file browser, editor and interface to chess
engines supporting the XBoard protocol. It supports SAN move format with
annotations including RAV, NAG, single and multi-line comments along with the
FEN tag which can be edited by arranging pieces on the board. It can also
validate and save to (optionally reduced) PGN format. Multiple games
(human/human, human/engine, engine/engine, human/ollama) can be loaded, created,
searched and edited. You can also customize the key bindings and create macros.

Since there are many variations of the curses library, Llama Chess requires
NCurses to be installed to make porting to other operating systems
easier. There are known issues with Sun/Solaris and NetBSD, although
Llama Chess has been tested to work well on OpenBSD, FreeBSD and Linux.

Build with CMake (see INSTALL for details). Requires a built sibling
libviper (VDK) checkout:

     (cd ../libviper && cmake . && make)
     cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build
     sudo cmake --install build

After installing, type "llama-chess -h" for command-line options or just
"llama-chess". Press F10 for the top menu bar (File, Game, Play, …). Help is
Ctrl-H (or F1 if your terminal does not capture it). Press "a" for About.

If you want PERL support you'll need to have libperl installed and pass
-DWITH_LIBPERL=ON to cmake. When compiled with PERL support, pressing ^O
will "filter" the current game through a PERL subroutine defined in
~/.llama-chess/perl.pl. The subroutine is passed a single argument which is a
generated FEN tag of the current game. The subroutine should return a FEN tag
after processing.

The latest version can be found at https://github.com/TragicWarrior/llama-chess.
If you find a bug or have a patch to fix one or a feature request you can use
the issue tracker there or send me an email.

There is also a public GIT repository available. Anonymous checkouts can be
done by doing:

    git clone https://github.com/TragicWarrior/llama-chess.git

Credits
-------

Llama Chess is based on CBoard, written by Ben Kibbey
<bjk@luxsci.net> (Jabber: bjk@jabber.org). The original CBoard sources,
issues, and wiki remain at https://gitlab.com/bjk/cboard.

See THANKS for additional contributors.
